.TH WTWM 1 "2026-08-18" "wtwm 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wtwm \- run the twm-compatible Wayland compositor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wtwm
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-f " twmrc" ]
.RB [ \-s " command" ]
.RB [ \-\-visual-mode " mode" ]
.RB [ \-\-diagnostic-dump " path" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wtwm
is a wlroots-based Wayland compositor that implements the observable window
management and appearance of X11 twm where the Wayland protocols permit it.
It manages native xdg-shell and Xwayland windows in one stack and draws classic
server-side title bars, menus, icons, and icon managers.
.PP
The executable, state directory, and login entry are named
.BR wtwm .
Installing wtwm does not replace
.BR twm (1),
an Xorg session, another Wayland compositor, or the display manager's default
session.  The packaged login entry is displayed as
.BR "Wayland twm" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Enable verbose wlroots and compositor logging.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
.TQ
.BI \-\-config " file"
Try the named twm configuration first.  See
.BR wtwmrc (5)
for normal search order and fallback behavior.
.TP
.BI \-s " command"
.TQ
.BI \-\-startup " command"
Run one command after runtime initialization and Wayland socket creation.
Configuration and startup failures that occur before this point prevent the
command from running.  Shell metacharacters select execution through
.BR sh (1);
simple commands are executed directly.
.TP
.BI \-\-visual-mode " mode"
Select
.BR color ,
.BR grayscale ,
or
.BR monochrome .
The default is
.BR color .
.TP
.BI \-\-diagnostic-dump " path"
Enable a bounded JSON state dump at
.IR path .
Sending
.B SIGUSR2
writes current outputs, managed windows, focus, cursor coordinates, and
resource counts.  The file is created with mode 0600; symbolic links and
non-regular targets are rejected.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print command-line usage and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
With no
.B \-f
argument, wtwm tries
.IR $HOME/.twmrc.0 ,
then
.IR $HOME/.twmrc ,
then a package-enabled generated system configuration,
then the packaged
.IR system.twmrc .
If none is readable, compiled-in defaults are used.  Existing user files are
read in place and are never rewritten by install, upgrade, reload, or removal.
.PP
Check a file before logging in with:
.PP
.EX
wtwm-config "$HOME/.twmrc"
.EE
.PP
Configuration is compatible with twm syntax.  Native applications are matched
by title and Wayland
.BR app_id ;
Xwayland applications are matched by
.B WM_NAME
and both parts of
.BR WM_CLASS .
See
.BR wtwmrc (5)
and the installed migration guide for translations of X11-only behavior.
.SH WAYLAND TRANSLATION INVENTORY
The following stable identifiers name every intentional boundary where native
Wayland cannot reproduce the literal X11 mechanism.  Xwayland retains the X11
path where stated; the compatibility guide provides the complete behavior and
test rationale for each identifier.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:compositor-owned-icons]
X icon windows and pixmaps become compositor-owned icon scenes because
rootless Xwayland cannot transplant arbitrary X windows into a Wayland scene.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:compositor-owned-menus]
twm menus become a compositor-owned scene layer because the compositor owns
Wayland composition rather than creating X root-child menu windows.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:compositor-owned-refresh]
Refresh actions damage compositor scene content because native clients cannot
receive X root-window exposure events.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:cut-buffer-clipboard]
The persistent X cut buffer is mirrored through Wayland
.B CLIPBOARD
because native Wayland has no root cut-buffer property.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:embedded-rootless-xwayland]
X11 clients run through a compositor-owned rootless Xwayland server rather
than an existing X screen root.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:fractional-scale-rounding]
Fractional output coordinates use deterministic shared-edge rounding because
Wayland scaling uses logical coordinates and 120ths rather than X pixels.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:identify-version-log]
.B f.identify
and
.B f.version
write to the compositor log because Wayland has no server-owned information
window.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:in-process-restart]
.BR f.restart ,
.BR f.source ,
and
.B f.twmrc
use one atomic in-process transaction so accepted Wayland resources survive.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:legacy-bell-priority]
Native Wayland has no compositor bell request or twm XSync priority control;
the documented conditional behavior is retained for Xwayland.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:logical-seat-aggregation]
Physical input devices are aggregated into one logical Wayland seat rather
than exposed through twm's direct X device model.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:native-close-only]
Native
.B f.delete
and
.B f.destroy
both issue xdg-shell close requests because the protocol cannot forcibly
disconnect one arbitrary client.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:native-colormap-noop]
.B f.colormap
is a verified native no-op because true-color Wayland buffers expose no
installed-colormap protocol.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:native-initial-placement]
Native initial placement selects an output from pointer or parent state because
xdg-shell has no X position hints or blocking root placement grab.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:native-popup-hierarchy]
Native application popups retain their xdg parent hierarchy rather than acting
as independent X override-redirect root children.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:native-size-constraints]
Native resizing uses the minimum and maximum constraints exposed by xdg-shell;
base, increment, and aspect hints remain X11-only.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:native-window-identity]
Native window-list matching maps X name, instance, and class identity to xdg
title and
.BR app_id .
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:output-root-spatial-model]
Each enabled output box is a spatial root equivalent whose background is
painted by the compositor; Wayland outputs are not independent X screens.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:rootless-cross-protocol-stacking]
Native popups and Xwayland override-redirect surfaces share a map-ordered
overlay because X sibling requests cannot name native surfaces.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:saved-state-snapshot]
Session restoration uses an explicit compositor-owned snapshot because native
Wayland has no
.B WM_SAVE_YOURSELF
or X session-manager property protocol.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:screen-zero-global-config]
The screen-zero configuration applies globally because Wayland outputs are not
separate X screens with independently parsed root configurations.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:selection-bridge]
Wayland
.B CLIPBOARD
and
.B PRIMARY
offers bridge to X11 selections under Wayland seat-focus and serial rules.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:startwm-in-process]
A supported
.B f.startwm
self-handoff becomes an atomic in-process restart because Wayland client
resources cannot transfer to an arbitrary replacement compositor.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:unicode-font-fallback]
Non-ASCII decoration text falls back from X core fonts to bounded Pango
rendering because native Wayland has no X core font protocol.
.TP
.B [wayland-translation:x11-resource-noops]
.BR NoBackingStore ,
.BR NoSaveUnders ,
and
.B NoGrabServer
are verified no-ops because their X server resources do not exist in a Wayland
scene graph.
.SH SESSION USE
For a real login, select
.B Wayland twm
in the display manager.  The
.B wtwm-session
wrapper starts exactly one compositor, forwards termination signals, records a
private log, and returns the compositor's status to the display manager.  It
does not restart wtwm or the display manager.  A normal exit or a failed start
therefore returns control to the login manager.
.PP
For testing inside an existing Wayland session, use a nested backend, for
example:
.PP
.EX
WLR_BACKENDS=wayland wtwm -s xterm
.EE
.PP
Do not select a DRM backend from an SSH shell.  DRM login testing requires an
active local logind session and a usable DRM device.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Locates user configuration and, when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset, compositor state and the session log.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Required by Wayland.  The login-session wrapper rejects a missing,
non-directory, or non-writable value.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Overrides the base directory for persistent state and
.IR wtwm/session.log .
.TP
.B WTWM_SYSTEM_CONFIG
Overrides the generated and compiled-in packaged configuration paths with one
test path.  Intended for builds and tests; normal user configuration belongs in
.IR ~/.twmrc .
.TP
.B WLR_BACKENDS
.TQ
.B WLR_RENDERER
wlroots backend and renderer selection.  These are useful for controlled
headless or nested testing.  A normal login should let the session choose its
platform backend.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.twmrc.0
First user configuration tried for screen zero.
.TP
.I $HOME/.twmrc
General user configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/share/wtwm/system.twmrc
Packaged fallback configuration for a default-prefix install.
.TP
.I /etc/wtwm/system.twmrc
Debian application-menu configuration generated by
.BR update-menus (8)
and preferred over the static fallback.  A user configuration still takes
precedence and is not merged with this file.
.TP
.I /etc/wtwm/system.twmrc-menu
Package-managed template used to generate the Debian menu configuration.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/wtwm/state
State explicitly written by the
.B f.saveyourself
action.  Ordinary exit, failure, and signals do not replace it.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/wtwm/session.log
Private login-wrapper log.
.TP
.I /usr/share/wayland-sessions/wtwm.desktop
Distinct Wayland login entry.
.TP
.I diagnostic-dump-path
Optional private JSON state file selected by
.BR \-\-diagnostic-dump .
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGHUP ,
.B SIGINT ,
.B SIGQUIT ,
and
.B SIGTERM
request orderly compositor termination.  The login wrapper forwards each as
.B SIGTERM
to its compositor child and waits for that child to be reaped.
.PP
.B SIGUSR2
writes the optional diagnostic state file without terminating the compositor.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates a normal compositor exit, including
.B f.quit
and orderly signal termination.  Invalid arguments return 2.  Configuration,
backend, renderer, socket, or other initialization failures return nonzero.
The login wrapper returns the same child status; 127 means it could not execute
the configured compositor binary.
.SH SECURITY
.B f.exec
and the
.B \-s
option execute commands with the user's privileges.  Review an imported
\&.twmrc before use.  Do not run wtwm or wtwm-session as root.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Use
.B \-d
and inspect
.I session.log
after a login failure.  Run
.B wtwm-config
separately to distinguish configuration errors from backend or device errors.
The installed troubleshooting guide covers blank displays, unavailable
Xwayland, fonts, permissions, recovery, and useful evidence to collect.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wtwm-config (1),
.BR wtwmrc (5),
.BR twm (1),
.BR Xwayland (1)
